<section xml:id="notes-week-06" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 6</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 6 of the course, from October 5 to October 9.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 10/5</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand symmetry of graphs and functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand the basic forms of certain function graphs.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use graph transformations to sketch graphs of more complicated functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Symmetry</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                What is symmetry outside of the mathematical context?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                What is symmetry outside of the mathematical context? Things being the same on both sides, left side being a mirror image of the right. Various planes of symmetry, common in nature.
            </p>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                In the mathematical context, we want to apply this to graphs, and in particular, to graphs of functions.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                In the mathematical context, we want to apply this to graphs, and in particular, to graphs of functions. While there are many different planes of symmetry in nature, there are only three we want to consider in this class: x-axis, y-axis, and origin.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For each of the following graphs, analyze the types of symmetry that they have: x-axis, y-axis, origin, or none.
