    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            What did we notice from those previous examples? Is there an algebraic way to determine this based on an equation (if you were given one)?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What did we notice from those previous examples? Is there an algebraic way to determine this based on an equation (if you were given one)? If <m>(x,y)</m> is on a graph, what other point needs to be on the graph for symmetry to work?
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="3in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Determine which types of symmetry each of the following equations have?
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>y^2 - x = 5</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>x^4 + y^2 = 10</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            What does this mean for functions of the form <m>y = f(x)</m>? How can we apply these previous observations?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What does this mean for functions of the form <m>y = f(x)</m>? How can we apply these previous observations? Get to definition of even and odd function.
        </p>

        <example workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the function <m>f(x) = x^4 + 3x - 1</m> even or odd? Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
        <solution component="a">
            <p>
                Neither.
            </p>
        </solution>
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Transformations of Graphs</title>
            
            <p>
                In this section, we study transformations of graphs, which is how we can take the graphs of a basic function and transform it into something more complicated. The basic functions we will consider are below.
            </p>
            <sidebyside>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-identity">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
<legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=x</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-square">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x^2 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=x^2</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
    </sidebyside>
    <sidebyside>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-squarert">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=sqrt(x) </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=\sqrt{x}</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-cubed">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x^3 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=x^3</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
                        <sidebyside>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-cubert">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x,y)=y^3 - x</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <implicit-curve at="f" function="f" k="0" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
                <image>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-recip">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=1/x </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=\frac{1}{x}</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>
                            </sidebyside>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="basic-functions-absval">
             <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=abs(x) </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f" stroke="blue"/>
      <legend anchor="(bbox[0], bbox[3])" alignment="se">
      <item ref="f"><m>y=|x|</m></item>
</legend>
  </coordinates>

</diagram>
  </prefigure>
                </image>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Vertical and Horizontal Translations</title>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            The first kind of transformations we want to consider are translations. If <m>g(x) = f(x) + 2</m>, how do the graphs of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> compare?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The first kind of transformations we want to consider are translations. If <m>g(x) = f(x) + 2</m>, how do the graphs of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> compare? If we're drawing these, it's <m>y = f(x)</m> or <m>y = g(x)</m>. So, the <m>y</m>-value for <m>g(x)</m> is increased by 2.  
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-transformation-vert">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-transformation-vert.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        This result can be summarized as follows:
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        This result can be summarized as follows: 
    </p>

    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        How would we adjust the <m>x</m>-value instead of the <m>y</m>-value?
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        How would we adjust the <m>x</m>-value instead of the <m>y</m>-value? We need to change the input, so something like <m>f(x-2)</m> instead.
    </p>
 
 <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-transformation-horiz">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-transformation-horiz.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>

</page>

<page>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
