<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Stretching and Shrinking</title>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            The other main things we can do with graphs is stretch or compress them in the vertical and horizontal directions.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The other main things we can do with graphs is stretch or compress them in the vertical and horizontal directions. This is going to be the result of multiplying numbers to either the <m>x</m> (input) or <m>y</m> (output) of the function. 
        </p>
    </paragraphs>
<sidebyside>
<image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-transformation-vertStretch">
            <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-transformation-vertStretch.xml" />
            </prefigure>
</image>
<image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-transformation-horizStretch">
            <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-transformation-horizStretch.xml" />
            </prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</page>

<page>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        There's a little bit more to this picture here:
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        There's a little bit more to this picture here: What if the coefficient is negative?
    </p>

    <image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-transformation-stretchReflect">
            <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-transformation-stretchReflect.xml" />
            </prefigure>
</image>

<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    <nbsp />
</p>
<p component="a">
    If the coefficient is negative, we also have a reflection. So reflect and stretch or compress.
</p>
    
</page>
